# The published short links, and one alias.
#
# What is left here is exactly what Blume's own `redirects` config cannot express.
# It bases every redirect against `basePath`, which is `/docs` — so a native
# `from: '/github'` is matched at `/docs/github`, and there is no way to opt out.
# Every rule below is root-level, so every one of them has to be a platform file.
# Blume documents this arrangement: "a `_redirects` or `vercel.json` you ship in
# `public/` is left untouched."
#
# The retired API-reference URLs moved OUT of this file into `blume.config.ts`,
# because those are under `/docs` and so are expressible natively — and `blume
# audit`'s link checker reads that list, while it cannot see this file.
#
# 302 throughout, never 301. A 301 is cached by the browser more or less forever,
# and every social link points at a third-party account that can move.
#
# `/install` is deliberately absent: it is a real script at `public/install`,
# served as a static asset. A redirect would break `curl -fsS` without `-L`.

/github         https://github.com/sigiroai              302
/x              https://x.com/SigiroAI                   302
/twitter        https://x.com/SigiroAI                   302
/linkedin       https://www.linkedin.com/company/sigiro  302
/discord        https://discord.gg/sigiro                302

# `@astrojs/sitemap` writes `sitemap-index.xml` and has no option to collapse it
# into one file, but `/sitemap.xml` is the path Search Console holds — and the path
# `agent-readability.json` advertises, since Blume derives that from `seo.sitemap`
# rather than from whatever generator actually ran.
/sitemap.xml    /sitemap-index.xml                       302

# The API description lives on the API, which is the only thing that can keep it
# honest. This site has never held a copy and should not start — but `/openapi.json`
# is the path a reader guesses, and it answered 404 to every one of them, including
# the assistants the footer sends here. A 302 costs nothing and cannot drift.
#
# 301, not 302, and the reason is a machine reader rather than a browser.
# integrations.sh probes this exact path, followed the 302, and recorded a SECOND
# HTTP surface at `sigiro.com` with its spec set to this URL — the same API listed
# twice, one entry pointing at a redirect. A permanent redirect states that
# `api.sigiro.com/openapi.json` is the canonical location rather than one of two
# equal addresses, which is what `/.well-known/integrations.json` also declares.
/openapi.json   https://api.sigiro.com/openapi.json      301
